WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.419
Think about your morning. You get up, wash, then get dressed, in order. That is sequence. If it is raining, you take an umbrella. That is selection. You keep brushing your teeth until they are clean. That is iteration.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:25.712
Selection means the program chooses a path using a condition. In pseudocode it starts with IF. If rain is TRUE, one message is shown. Otherwise, the ELSE message is shown.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:39.825
In a flowchart, look at the arrows. A decision diamond whose paths both carry on down is selection. If an arrow goes back up to an earlier step, the steps repeat. That is iteration, also called a loop.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:56.251
There are three kinds of loop. A count-controlled loop, FOR, repeats a fixed number of times. A pre-conditioned loop, WHILE, tests first, so it may run zero times. A post-conditioned loop, REPEAT UNTIL, tests last, so it always runs at least once.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:07.491
First, look for the keyword or the shape. IF, or a diamond with no loop back, is selection. FOR, WHILE, REPEAT, or an arrow going back up, is iteration.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:17.095
For the kind of loop, check where the condition is. FOR is count-controlled. WHILE at the top is pre-conditioned. UNTIL at the bottom is post-conditioned.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:25.522
For a why this loop question, find what makes the loop stop. Then give the benefit, and link it to that stopping condition with because.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:40.730
Here is a real question from Paper 1 in June 2025. Lines ten to fifteen use a post-conditioned loop. Explain one reason why it is better than a count-controlled loop. It is worth two marks.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:55.252
Each pass adds one to testNum, then checks whether testNum divides the number exactly. If the remainder is zero, flag becomes TRUE. The loop stops when testNum reaches the number, or when flag is TRUE.

00:01:55.850 --> 00:02:11.103
Try number equals nine. Pass one: testNum is two, and nine mod two is one, so flag stays FALSE. Pass two: testNum is three, and nine mod three is zero, so flag becomes TRUE. The loop stops after only two passes.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:20.148
A count-controlled loop from two to nine would always make eight passes, even after the answer is known. That is six wasted passes.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:34.609
So the answer is: it is more efficient, because the loop ends as soon as a factor of the number is found, while a count-controlled loop would keep going. This explanation is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:44.454
A common mistake is calling a WHILE loop count-controlled because it uses a counter. It tests a condition, so it is condition-controlled.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:50.981
Another mistake is a benefit with no link to the algorithm. Say what makes this loop stop early.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:03:00.868
Now you try. Name the kind of loop used. Then explain one reason it is better here than a count-controlled loop. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:08.933
The keyword is WHILE, and the condition is tested at the top, before each pass. So it is a pre-conditioned loop.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:22.215
Ava is the second name, so found becomes TRUE on pass two and the loop stops. A count-controlled loop would check all six names. So it is more efficient, because it stops as soon as the name is found.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:32.485
Remember the method: spot the keyword or shape, find where the test is, then give the benefit with because. Now try the practice questions for this type.
